Inter-Component Contract Gate

Status: Normative SDLC2 development rule
Applies to: Any sprint that creates or changes communication across component boundaries.

Rule

Implementation across a component boundary MUST NOT begin until an intended interface contract exists and has been reviewed and approved.

A sprint involving only one self-contained component does not trigger this gate.

Purpose

Components must not independently invent opposite sides of an interface during implementation.

The boundary between components is part of the architecture and MUST be treated as an explicit engineering artifact.

Sprint-Start Gate

Before coding begins, identify every component boundary affected by the sprint.

For each affected boundary, an intended contract MUST define at least:

* participating components and their responsibilities;
* direction and purpose of communication;
* communication mechanism or protocol;
* principal operations or interactions;
* information crossing the boundary;
* expected success and failure semantics;
* intended authoritative contract artifact.

The contract may initially be high-level.

Unknown implementation details MAY remain unresolved when they do not prevent both components from sharing the same architectural intent.

Contract Artifacts

Use the most appropriate machine-readable artifact when practical, including:

* Protocol Buffers for gRPC;
* OpenAPI for HTTP APIs;
* JSON Schema for structured documents;
* event/message schemas for asynchronous communication;
* database schemas for shared persistence boundaries.

A concise Markdown specification is acceptable when a more formal representation is not yet justified.

During Implementation

The contract MAY evolve as implementation reveals new information.

Changes MUST be made explicitly to the contract rather than independently inferred by participating components.

Implementation agents MUST NOT silently invent, reinterpret, or diverge from an approved interface.

Ambiguity affecting component interoperability MUST return to contract definition before implementation continues.

Code-Review Gate

Code review MUST verify implementation against the resulting interface contract.

Review includes both sides of the boundary and checks contract conformance, compatibility, errors, identifiers, security assumptions, and relevant failure behavior.

Sprint-Close Gate

The contract MUST represent the interface that actually shipped.

A sprint MUST NOT close with undocumented contract drift between the approved contract and implementation.

Lifecycle Invariant

Sprint planning establishes interface intent. Implementation evolves it explicitly. Code review verifies conformance. Sprint closure preserves the shipped contract.